<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Baptiste Bagaza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1946–2016</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Baptiste Bagaza was a Burundian army officer who seized power in a coup in November 1976 and ruled as president until 1987. He pursued development projects but clashed with the Catholic Church, restricting religious activity and expelling missionaries. He was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-buyoya">Pierre Buyoya</a> while abroad in September 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-bagaza.jpg" alt="Jean-Baptiste Bagaza" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Louis Debaize · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Baptiste_Bagaza_-_1978.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
